{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And I charged your judges at that time, saying.</b> What is 'saying'? To whom would the judges say this? And 'at that time' is entirely superfluous. And it appears that he warned them against oppression of justice — rather, 'at that time, saying': at that time when it is clear to the judge which way the judgment inclines, he should immediately say: 'So-and-so, you are innocent; so-and-so, you are liable.' And what Rashi explained, 'from here: be deliberate in judgment' — perhaps Rashi too holds that its meaning is to warn against oppression of justice, but in any case they infer from this also to be deliberate in judgment; for if not, the verse should be silent on this matter — do we then deal with wicked judges who oppress justice for no reason? Rather, certainly every judge must be deliberate in judgment, and there is reason to fear that through this he may come to oppression of justice and delay more than is proper. Therefore he said: 'at that time, saying.' And it could be that Rashi's opinion is to explain 'and I charged' in the manner of what is said in the portion of Va'etchanan (Deuteronomy 4:23): 'And you shall make for yourselves a likeness of all that HaShem your Elohim has commanded you' — Rashi explained: 'has commanded you' — not to make. And 'commanded you' would be an expression of warning, that He warned you not to make. So too 'and I charged your judges' is an expression of warning, meaning: I warned you concerning 'at that time, saying' — that he should not cut the judgment hastily at that time, but should be deliberate in judgment. And this is a sound interpretation from the standpoint of the language."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:01.797258948Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}